.docname {Tenancy Service}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Domain | A space that holds members and buildings, one per organization or part of one | `northfield` |
    | Subdomain | A domain with a parent domain | `northfield-east` under `northfield` |
    | Membership | One account in one domain, with one role | Ada, `northfield`, `business_admin` |
    | Role ladder | The roles and their weights, from `roles.json` | `admin` 100, `business_admin` 80, `business_staff` 60, `standard_customer` 10 |
    | Manager | A member with `business_admin` or higher | Ada |
    | Join policy | Who may join on their own | `invite-only`, `open-via-idp` |
    | Invite code | A one-use code that adds its holder to a domain with a set role | 32 hex characters, 7 days |
    | Claims header | The signed-in caller's identity, put on the request by the edge | `x-gateway-claims` |
    | HMAC signature | A code from a shared secret and the request body that proves the caller is another service | `X-Signature` |
    | Cedar | The policy language access rules are written in | `CanManageDomain` |

Go service at `backend/tenancy`, with its own PostgreSQL database. It owns domains, memberships and invite codes. It never mints a token and never talks to Keycloak: [`claims-gateway`](claims-gateway.qd) reads memberships from here and puts them in the token. Domain side: [Identity & Tenancy](../architecture/auth-architecture.qd).

- Makes no outbound call. Every exchange is someone calling in.
- Two kinds of caller: a person through the edge (claims header), or another service (HMAC).

---

## Package layout

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
| `cmd/tenancy/main.go` | Config, migrations, pool, routes, clean shutdown |
| `internal/config/config.go` | Env vars, and `CV_TENANCY` |
| `internal/api/handler.go` | `Mount`, every handler, `writeErr` (error to status) |
| `internal/service/tenancy.go` | Rules: who may join, grant ceilings, outranking, last manager |
| `internal/store/store.go` | `Domain`, `Membership`, `InviteCode`, the `Store` interface |
| `internal/store/postgres.go` | `Store` on PostgreSQL with `pgx` |
| `internal/storefake/fake.go` | `Store` in memory, for tests |
| `migrations/` | `0001_init` domains and memberships, `0002_subdomains`, `0003_invite_codes`, `0004_public_domains` |

Libraries: [Auth Contracts](../packages/auth-contracts.qd) (HMAC check, role ladder), [Auth Middleware](../packages/auth-middleware.qd) (`RequireMeshClaims`), [Auth Policy](../packages/auth-policy.qd) (`CanManageDomain`).

---

## What it stores

| Table | Columns | Rules |
|---|---|---|
| `domains` | `id`, `name`, `display_name`, `sso_config`, `join_policy`, `parent_id`, `is_public`, `created_at` | `name` unique. `join_policy` is `invite-only` (default) or `open-via-idp`. Deleting a parent deletes its subdomains. `sso_config` is never read or written |
| `memberships` | `account_id`, `domain_id`, `role`, `external_id`, `joined_via`, `created_at` | Key is (`account_id`, `domain_id`): one role per account per domain. `joined_via` is `self-idp`, `invite` or `local`. Deleted with the domain |
| `invite_codes` | `id`, `domain_id`, `code`, `role`, `created_by`, `expires_at`, `redeemed_by`, `redeemed_at`, `created_at` | `code` unique. Deleted with the domain |

- `account_id` is the Keycloak user id. It is **not** a foreign key: the account lives in Keycloak's database.
- `role` is free text in the table. The service checks it against the role ladder before writing.

---

## Routes

Mounted only when `CV_TENANCY` is not `disabled`. Otherwise only `/health` exists.

### For people (claims header)

`RequireMeshClaims` decodes `x-gateway-claims` (base64 JSON). Missing, not base64, not JSON, or no `sub`: `401`.

| Method, path | Extra check | Does | Answers |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /domains` | none | Public domains, with member counts, sorted by name | `200` |
| `POST /domains` | none | New root domain. The caller becomes its `business_admin` | `201`, `400`, `409` |
| `GET /me/memberships` | none | The caller's memberships | `200` |
| `POST /domains/{domain}/join` | none | Join an `open-via-idp` domain. Role at most `standard_customer` | `204`, `400`, `403`, `404` |
| `POST /domains/{domain}/invite` | `CanManageDomain` | Add or change a known account. Role at most `business_admin` | `204`, `400`, `403`, `404`, `409` |
| `DELETE /domains/{domain}/members/{accountId}` | self, or `CanManageDomain` | Leave, or remove someone | `204`, `400`, `403`, `404`, `409` |
| `GET /domains/{domain}/subdomains` | none | Direct children only | `200`, `404` |
| `POST /domains/{domain}/subdomains` | `CanManageDomain` on the parent | New child domain | `201`, `400`, `403`, `404`, `409` |
| `POST /domains/{domain}/invite-codes` | `CanManageDomain` | New code. Role at most `business_admin` | `201`, `400`, `403`, `404` |
| `POST /invite-codes/{code}/redeem` | none | Use a code | `204`, `400`, `403` |

### For services (HMAC)

| Method, path | Caller | Does | Answers |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /internal/memberships?accountId=` | `claims-gateway`, at every sign-in and refresh | An account's memberships | `200`, `400`, `403` |
| `POST /internal/provision` | `claims-gateway`, first sign-in | Join with the role the identity provider gave. The only way to get `admin` | `204`, `400`, `403`, `404` |
| `POST /internal/domains` | `provisioner` | Make an organization's domain, or return the existing one | `201`, `409` on a race |

### Error to status

`writeErr`, `internal/api/handler.go`:

| Service error | Status |
|---|---|
| `ErrDomainNotFound` | `404` |
| `ErrInviteOnly`, `ErrRoleNotGrantable`, `ErrMemberOutranksActor` | `403` |
| `ErrDomainNameTaken`, `ErrLastAdminCannotLeave` | `409` |
| `ErrInviteCodeInvalid`, `ErrUnknownRole` | `400` |
| anything else | `500 internal error` |

The shapes the frontend reads (domain listing, `/me/memberships`, create and join bodies, invite-code reply) are pinned in `schemas/fixtures/tenancy-domains.json`. `internal/api/wire_test.go` and `wire_routes_test.go` replay it here; `frontend/src/utils/domains/domains.spec.ts` replays it in the browser code.

---

## Sign-in reads memberships

The most frequent call. Every sign-in and every refresh in `claims-gateway` makes it.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant G as claims-gateway<br/>tenancyclient.MembershipsFor
        participant R as RequireSignature
        participant H as internalMemberships
        participant S as service.MembershipsFor
        participant DB as PostgreSQL
        G->>R: GET /internal/memberships?accountId=a1b2…<br/>X-Signature over empty body
        R->>R: Verify(secret, "", signature)
        R->>H: next
        H->>H: accountId present and a UUID?
        H->>S: MembershipsFor(a1b2…)
        S->>DB: memberships join domains where account_id
        DB-->>S: rows
        S->>S: map to authcontracts.Membership
        H-->>G: 200 [{domain, role, externalId}]

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `accountId=a1b2c3d4-0000-4000-8000-000000000001`, header `52a0b3ef…` (fixture secret, empty body) |
| 4 | Not a UUID, like `ada`: `400 accountId must be a UUID`, before any query |
| 7 | Rows: (`a1b2…`, domain `9e…`, name `northfield`, `business_admin`, external `""`, `local`) |
| 8 | `Membership{Domain:"northfield", Role:"business_admin"}` |
| 9 | `[{"domain":"northfield","role":"business_admin"}]`. `externalId` left out when empty. No memberships: `[]` |

---

## Create a domain (person)

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant E as Edge
        participant H as createOwnDomain
        participant S as service.CreateOwnDomain
        participant DB as PostgreSQL
        B->>E: POST /tenancy/domains {name, displayName, isPublic}
        E->>H: x-gateway-claims added
        H->>H: name and displayName not empty?
        H->>S: CreateOwnDomain(AccountID = claims.sub, …)
        S->>DB: DomainByName(name)
        DB-->>S: not found
        S->>S: pick join policy
        S->>DB: insert into domains
        S->>DB: UpsertMembership(caller, business_admin, local)
        H-->>B: 201 {id, name, displayName, joinPolicy, isPublic}

Join policy when the body has none:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["joinPolicy in body?"] -- yes --> K["keep it"]
        A -- no --> P{"isPublic?"}
        P -- yes --> O["open-via-idp"]
        P -- no --> I["invite-only"]

- Name already there at step 5: `409 domain name is already taken`.
- Another request inserts the same name between steps 5 and 8: the unique constraint fires, and the service still answers `409`.
- The new token does not yet hold the new membership. The browser calls `/gateway/refresh` to get one that does.

**Example.** Ada posts `{"name":"northfield","displayName":"Northfield Library","isPublic":true}`. The domain gets `open-via-idp`, Ada gets `business_admin`, the answer is `201` with `"isPublic":true`. `memberCount` is left out because it is only filled in the public listing.

### Create a domain (provisioner)

`POST /internal/domains`, `service.CreateDomain`, differs on purpose:

1. Name already there: return the **existing** domain with `201`. Running a reconcile tick twice must not fail.
2. Empty `joinPolicy` becomes `invite-only`.
3. Insert. A race on the unique name becomes `409`, which `provisioner` also counts as success.
4. No membership is created. The organization has no first admin from this call.

---

## Grant ceilings

Each route can grant only up to a set role. `checkGrantable(role, ceiling)`:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        R["role asked"] --> L{"on the role ladder?"}
        L -- no --> U["400 unknown role"]
        L -- yes --> C{"weight above the ceiling?"}
        C -- yes --> G["403 role is above what this route may grant"]
        C -- no --> OK["allowed"]

| Route | Ceiling | Empty role |
|---|---|---|
| `join` | `standard_customer` (10) | becomes `standard_customer` |
| `invite` | `business_admin` (80) | `400` |
| `invite-codes`, create | `business_admin` (80) | `400` |
| `redeem` | `business_admin` (80), checked again on the stored code | not possible |
| `/internal/provision` | none: trusts the role `claims-gateway` sends | stored as is |

`CanManageDomain` already requires `business_admin`, so a manager never grants more than their own role.

**Example.** Bob, `business_admin` of `northfield`, invites Carla as `admin`: weight 100 > 80, `403`. As `business_staff`: allowed.

---

## Invite and remove

### Invite end to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Bob's browser
        participant H as inviteMember
        participant P as authpolicy
        participant S as service.Invite
        participant DB as PostgreSQL
        B->>H: POST /domains/northfield/invite {accountId, role}
        H->>P: CanManageDomain(claims.memberships, northfield)
        P-->>H: true
        H->>H: accountId a UUID?
        H->>S: Invite(…, ActorRole = roleIn(claims, northfield))
        S->>S: checkGrantable(role, business_admin)
        S->>DB: DomainByName, then MembersOf
        S->>S: target outranks Bob?
        S->>S: lowering the last manager?
        S->>DB: UpsertMembership(role, invite)
        H-->>B: 204

`roleIn` takes the **highest** role the claims hold in that domain. The checks after step 7, in order:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["target's current role"] --> O{"weight above Bob's?"}
        O -- yes --> E1["403 member holds a higher role than you"]
        O -- no --> M{"new role below business_admin<br/>and target is the only manager?"}
        M -- yes --> E2["409 cannot remove the last admin"]
        M -- no --> W["upsert: insert, or replace role"]

- Equal weights pass: a `business_admin` may change another `business_admin`.
- The upsert replaces role, `external_id` and `joined_via` on an existing membership.

### Leave or remove

`DELETE /domains/{domain}/members/{accountId}`:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["accountId a UUID?"] -- no --> E0["400"]
        A -- yes --> S{"accountId is the caller?"}
        S -- yes --> L["Leave"]
        S -- no --> C{"CanManageDomain?"}
        C -- no --> E1["403 forbidden"]
        C -- yes --> R["Remove: target outranks caller?"]
        R -- yes --> E2["403"]
        R -- no --> D
        L --> D{"target is the last manager?"}
        D -- yes --> E3["409"]
        D -- no --> X["delete membership, 204"]

**Example.** `northfield` has Ada (`business_admin`) and Bob (`business_staff`).

- Ada leaves: she is the only manager, `409`.
- Ada invites Bob as `business_admin`, then leaves: two managers, `204`.
- Bob removes Ada while still `business_staff`: no `CanManageDomain`, `403`.

`Remove` takes the caller's role as a required argument, so an empty role cannot skip the rank check by accident. `Leave` takes none: a person may always leave, unless they are the last manager.

---

## Invite codes

### Create

1. `CanManageDomain`, else `403`.
2. `checkGrantable(role, business_admin)`.
3. Domain must exist, else `404`.
4. 16 random bytes from `crypto/rand`, written as 32 hex characters.
5. Expires 7 days from now.
6. Answer `201 {"code":"3f9a…","role":"business_staff","expiresAt":"2026-11-09T10:00:00Z"}`.

### Redeem end to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Carla's browser
        participant H as redeemInviteCode
        participant S as service.RedeemInviteCode
        participant DB as PostgreSQL
        B->>H: POST /invite-codes/3f9a…/redeem
        H->>S: RedeemInviteCode(code, claims.sub)
        S->>DB: one statement: update invite_codes … returning, joined to domains
        DB-->>S: row, or none
        S->>S: checkGrantable(code role, business_admin)
        S->>DB: UpsertMembership(role, invite)
        H-->>B: 204

Step 3 is one SQL statement:

```sql
with redeemed as (
  update invite_codes set redeemed_by = $2, redeemed_at = now()
  where code = $1 and redeemed_by is null and expires_at > now()
  returning id, domain_id, role, created_by, expires_at
)
select … from redeemed join domains d on d.id = redeemed.domain_id
```

- The `where` marks the code used only if it is unused and not expired. PostgreSQL locks the row, so of two people redeeming at once, exactly one gets a row back.
- No row: `400` invite code invalid. Covers unknown, used and expired alike.
- Step 5 fails: the code is already spent and no membership is made.

**Example.** Code made 2026-11-02 10:00, expires 2026-11-09 10:00. Carla redeems on the 5th: member. Dan tries the same code a minute later: `400`.

---

## Join and provision

Both end in `service.Join`:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["SelfJoin: role empty becomes standard_customer,<br/>checkGrantable(role, standard_customer)"] --> J
        P["/internal/provision: accountId a UUID?"] --> J["Join"]
        J --> D{"domain exists?"}
        D -- no --> E1["404"]
        D -- yes --> O{"join_policy open-via-idp?"}
        O -- no --> E2["403 domain is invite-only"]
        O -- yes --> W["upsert membership, joined_via self-idp"]

`/internal/provision` gets `{accountId, domainName, role, externalId}` from `claims-gateway`. `role` is the highest ladder role in the Keycloak token, `admin` included.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | required | PostgreSQL connection string |
| `INTERNAL_SIGNING_SECRET` | required | HMAC secret, the same in `claims-gateway` and `provisioner` |
| `ADDR` | `:3000` | Listen address |
| `CV_TENANCY` | enabled | `disabled` mounts no routes but `/health`. Any other value, or none, mounts them all |

Start-up: load config, run migrations (any error but "no change" stops the process), open the pool, listen. On `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM`, running requests get 10 s.

---

## Tests

```bash
just test tenancy               # unit, no database
just test tenancy-integration   # store against a real PostgreSQL, testcontainers
```

| File | Checks |
|---|---|
| `service/tenancy_test.go` | Create, subdomains, public listing, join policy defaults, invite codes, join, leave, last manager |
| `service/grants_test.go` | Every ceiling: `admin` refused, off-ladder `400`, empty self-join role |
| `service/outrank_test.go` | A member above the caller cannot be changed or removed; equal is fine |
| `api/handler_test.go` | Every route: status codes, claims required, `CanManageDomain` gates, UUID checks |
| `api/grants_test.go`, `api/outrank_test.go` | The same rules seen through HTTP |
| `api/wire_test.go`, `api/wire_routes_test.go` | Bodies match `schemas/fixtures/tenancy-domains.json` |
| `store/postgres_test.go` | Behind `-tags=integration`. Real SQL: duplicate name, cascades on parent and domain delete, exactly one winner when two redeem at once, live member counts, idempotent upsert |

---

## Gaps

- **Deleted accounts keep their memberships.** Keycloak owns accounts and nothing tells `tenancy` when one is deleted. A comment in `0001_init.up.sql` names an `account.deleted` consumer in `internal/events`; that package does not exist.
- **`CV_TENANCY=disabled` breaks sign-in.** `/internal/memberships` disappears, `claims-gateway` gets `404`, and every sign-in answers `503`.
- **A domain from `provisioner` has no manager.** Nobody can invite or make invite codes until someone joins with `business_admin` or higher.
- **`GET /domains/{domain}/subdomains` checks no membership.** Any signed-in person can list any domain's children.
- **The HMAC signature on `GET /internal/memberships` does not cover `accountId`.** The body is empty, so the signature is the same for every account.
- **`sso_config` is never used.**
